#{[17592186304789 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,\\,5\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,\\,{u}^{2}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u(t)} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u(t)\\end{bmatrix}{u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304791 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(g∘u)^{′}(t)=g′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}0,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{u(t)},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}(t)}⟩{u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}⟨\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{1}{({e}^{2t})^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{2t}},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{4t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,-2{e}^{-2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304891 "Using the constant multiple and the sum rules, we have \n\n$$\n(2f(t)+3g(t))^{′}=2{f}^{′}(t)+3{g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}1\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ti-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2f(t)+3g(t))^{′} & =2{f}^{′}(t)+3{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)+3(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ti-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ti-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305066 "By the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u+1})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{2u})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{u+1}\\,i+2{e}^{2u}\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{u+1}\\,i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})=4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\ (f∘u)^{′}(t) & =({e}^{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,i+2{e}^{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)4t \\\\  & =4t{e}^{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,i+8t{e}^{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308444 "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+k \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+k)\\cdot (t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+t\\,k)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308623 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)f(t))^{′}=v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t+1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{2t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)f(t))^{′} & =v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t},\\,{2}^{t+1},\\,{2}^{2t}+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩-⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t+1}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323770 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323790 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323791 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323821 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=8\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 20\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 20\\sqrt{2} & =40\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{40} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323869 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2yz)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C.\n$$"] [17592186323875 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323878 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\,i-2xy\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323880 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323883 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}+1)\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326763 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t+2)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,dt \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-2(\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326819 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 4)-3(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 4)+0 \\\\  & =25+6 \\\\  & =31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|31|=31.\n$$"] [17592186326911 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326925 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)-2(3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2k-2+6 \\\\  & =-2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ -2k+4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$"] [17592186326926 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 1)+2(2\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 1)+(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-22+20+4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|2|=2.\n$$"] [17592186326928 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329720 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}} & =\\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329721 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15}{30} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332914 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACD$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/a6/a60b049aef53aef28802b084b3e445fd6253978500ca981d3b799977523cdd71.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACD,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(8)(5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{55}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈16.383,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACD} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 16.383 \\\\  & =32.766.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $33.$"] [17592186335769 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186335939 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/68/68f5708d49577befe9bac6a7aaf6a54fcdb30dcd344367d2c69b8924093dbb61.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 14\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{42.1}^{∘} \\\\  & =84\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{42.1}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈56.315\\,8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{103.8}^{∘} \\\\  & =17.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{103.8}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈16.994\\,8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =56.315\\,8+16.994\\,8 \\\\  & =73.310\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $73.3.$"] [17592186335940 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ACD$ and $△ABC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7e/7e3232258a523e4dbfebb6c380f5b88c2a54036b4d719e70ebf9060054f825d7.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ACD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =189\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈163.679,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△ABC:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 36\\cdot 30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{33}^{∘} \\\\  & =540\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{33}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈294.105,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ACD}+{A}_{ABC} \\\\  & =163.679+294.105 \\\\  & =457.784\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $458.$"] [17592186338805 "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(f(t)+4g(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+4{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+4g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+4{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,0⟩+4⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,0⟩+⟨4,\\,-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\,16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+4,\\,\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\,0+16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\,16{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338806 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t \\\\ {t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ {t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338918 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-y-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"] [17592186338919 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186338920 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}4t-1 \\\\ 3t+2 \\\\ 5t+6\\end{bmatrix}+(2t-3)\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8t-2 \\\\ 6t+4 \\\\ 10t+12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8t-12 \\\\ 6t-9 \\\\ 10t-15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16t-14 \\\\ 12t-5 \\\\ 20t-3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338921 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2t\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 8 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 16t \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2t+{t}^{2}){e}^{t} \\\\ 16t \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338922 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$"] [17592186341858 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & ≈53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341859 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈67.658,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$"] [17592186342070 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ t & t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3(t\\cdot 7-(-1)\\cdot 2)+6(t\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot t) \\\\  & =-21t-6+12t+6t \\\\  & =-3t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ -3t-6 & =0 \\\\ -3t & =6 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-2.$"] [17592186342071 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & 1 & t \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1))-(2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))+t(2\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-t-5+3t \\\\  & =2t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 2t-5 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =5 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [17592186342075 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i+j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =-3i \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =2i+1j \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+3k)-(i+j) \\\\  & =3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)-0+0 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"] [17592186342076 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i+2j+3k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+j+k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(-i+3j-2k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot (-4)-4\\cdot (-1))-3(0\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-1))+(0\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12+6+4 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|22|=22.\n$$"] [17592186342077 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and 